// THE ONLY IMPURE MODULE IN THE LIBRARY — the clocks, since cycle 0.3.0
// (`meta/roadmap/0.3/0.3.0.md`, TM-247 … TM-249). Governed by
// `meta/specs/HOST.md`.
//
// ============================ THE PURITY RULE ============================
// NO SYSCALL, NO CLOCK, NO ENVIRONMENT READ AND NO FILE READ EXISTS ANYWHERE
// IN THIS LIBRARY EXCEPT IN THIS DIRECTORY (TM-018, `SAFETY.md` §3).
//
// Everything outside `src/host/` is a pure function of its arguments. That is
// what makes the library reproducible, testable with no double, and portable
// by rewriting one module. `check_purity` fails the build when it stops being
// true, and it is the single most important check in the suite.
//
// AND THE ARROW POINTS ONE WAY: NOTHING imports `host` except `src/lib.npk`
// and an application (`BUILD.md` B-17). That is the purity boundary expressed
// as a layering rule, and `check_layering` enforces it.
// =========================================================================
//
// WHAT FILLS IT: five public functions and nothing else (H-1) — four since
// cycle 0.3.0: `host_now_utc`, `host_now_instant`, `host_now_boot` and
// `host_clock_res`, with `HostClock`, the argument H-1 names; and
// `host_system_zone`, since cycle 0.3.2, with `SystemZone` and `ZoneSource`,
// what it answers (TM-256). Beside them, nothing public: six numbers and five
// helpers -- `timespec_ns`, and the system zone's three steps and its
// not-found answer. `clock_gettime` and `clock_getres` are reached through
// `sys` with a 16-byte `timespec` laid out in a `buffer` (probe 03 pinned that
// shape), and the monotonic reading is the floor's `mono_now()` (H-5);
// `readlink` through `sys` into a `buffer` of `NTIME_PATH_MAX` bytes, and
// `/etc/timezone` through the floor's `open` and `read`, its descriptor an
// `OwnedFd` -- the library's one (`SAFETY.md` S-20).
// WHEN: cycle 0.3 (`meta/roadmap/0.3/README.md`) — the clocks at 0.3.0, the
// review of `check_purity`'s ban list against this body at 0.3.1, the system
// zone at 0.3.2 and the double at 0.3.3. (Until cycle 0.3.0 this file was the
// PLACEHOLDER of 0.0.1 P-7 and P-11, replaced and never deleted, and it said
// cycle 0.3 "is also when `check_purity` goes live and must be SEEN TO FAIL
// against a planted `mono_now()` outside this directory". Dated at cycle
// 0.2.4, cycle 0.2's close -- its audit's S4: `check_purity` has been live
// since cycle 0.0.3, TM-126, and the self-check plants a `mono_now()` outside
// this directory on every run; cycle 0.3 reviews its ban list against this
// module's body.)
//
// WHAT IT MAY IMPORT: `span` (the readings' types and their constructors:
// `Instant`'s and `Timestamp`'s fields are sealed, so only `span` builds them),
// `cal` (the range check's identity) and `core` (`BUILD.md` B-17, TM-247) — and
// `zone`, for the name lookup, when cycle 0.3.2 needs it. (Cycle 0.3.2,
// TM-256: it does not. `host_system_zone` answers the zone's NAME, and the
// lookup is the caller's, `zone`'s pure `zone_by_name` at cycle 0.6, so a
// program that reads the clock owes no zone arm.)
//
// THE ERRORS IT RAISES: none of its own. `cal`'s `ETimeValue`, relayed from
// `timestamp_of` (H-8's range check) or raised by `timespec_ns`; and the
// kernel's errno, FORWARDED VERBATIM (`SAFETY.md` S-5), exactly as
// `lib/nsys.npk` does — a dynamic operand, which arms nothing. A root that
// imports this module alone owes eleven identities, measured: `cal.ETimeValue`,
// the floor of six and the four arithmetic arms (`SAFETY.md` S-4). The system
// zone raises nothing and forwards nothing (TM-256): a step that cannot read
// its source has not answered, and its loops are `for` loops, which state no
// measure -- so the eleven stand.
mod:host;

use "../cal/cal.npk".ETimeValue;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_DURATION_NS_MAX;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_PATH_MAX;
use "../span/span.npk".Instant;
use "../span/span.npk".InstantClock;
use "../span/span.npk".Timestamp;
use "../span/span.npk".instant_of;
use "../span/span.npk".timestamp_of;

// The syscall numbers, x86-64 (TM-008), and the clock ids (H-4). 228 is the
// one the compiler's runtime calls in `npk_mono_now` at the pin (`git show
// 5fbaf4a:runtime/npkrt.ll`: `@npk_sys6(i64 228, i64 1, ...)`) and the kernel's
// table names `clock_gettime`; 229 is in no file of the compiler's tree, and
// the kernel's table names it `clock_getres` (`arch/x86/entry/syscalls/
// syscall_64.tbl`, `meta/research/CURRENCY.md`), its behaviour measured at
// cycle 0.3.0's planning: `{0 s, 1 ns}` for clocks 0, 1 and 7, and an errno in
// the `Result` for 99. 0 and 7 are H-4's, and `probe03` measures them apart on
// every run; 1 is the id the runtime's own call passes.
fixed int64:SYS_CLOCK_GETTIME = 228i64;
fixed int64:SYS_CLOCK_GETRES  = 229i64;
fixed int64:CLOCK_REALTIME    = 0i64;
fixed int64:CLOCK_MONOTONIC   = 1i64;
fixed int64:CLOCK_BOOTTIME    = 7i64;

// THE CLOCK `host_clock_res` IS ASKED ABOUT (H-1, TM-248). `host`'s own, three
// variants, because `span`'s `InstantClock` has no realtime clock and must not
// gain one: `instant_of(ns, InstantClock.Realtime)` would build an `Instant`
// from the wall clock under the type's own name, the hazard M-3 exists for.
#[derive(Eq, Clone, Debug, Copy)]
pub enum:HostClock = { Realtime; Monotonic; Boottime; };

// A `timespec`'s two fields as nanoseconds, or `ETimeValue` for a reading an
// `int64` of nanoseconds cannot hold -- checked BEFORE the multiplication, so
// nothing here traps (H-7's posture, `SAFETY.md` S-12). The range it answers
// in is 0 ... `NTIME_DURATION_NS_MAX`; outside it -- a negative second among
// it -- the detail is `ValueFault.Overflow`, and a nanosecond field outside one
// second is `NanoRange`, each written here until `meta/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md` O-X8
// says how a refusal hands its detail back. A kernel keeping its contract
// meets none of them: the boot clock counts up from zero and a resolution is
// a nanosecond here, so each is held by reading, not by a test (TM-249).
func:timespec_ns = int64(int64:secs, int64:nanos) {
    if (secs < 0i64)                  { fail ETimeValue; }     // Overflow
    if (nanos < 0i64)                 { fail ETimeValue; }     // NanoRange
    if (nanos >= NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC) { fail ETimeValue; }     // NanoRange
    int64:top = NTIME_DURATION_NS_MAX / NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC;
    if (secs > top)                   { fail ETimeValue; }     // Overflow
    if (secs == top) {                                         // Overflow
        if (nanos > (NTIME_DURATION_NS_MAX % NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC)) { fail ETimeValue; }
    }
    pass (secs * NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC) + nanos;
};

// `HOST.md` H-4, H-7 and H-8: `clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)`. The kernel's
// errno is forwarded verbatim; the reading becomes a `Timestamp` through
// `timestamp_of`, whose refusal of a `secs` outside the range (`YearRange`)
// and of a `nanos` outside one second (`NanoRange`) IS H-8's range check -- an
// unset machine clock is `ETimeValue`, not a value that fails later.
//
// THE TWO READS are of a 16-byte `buffer` at the offsets the `timespec` fixes,
// slot 0 the seconds and slot 1 the nanoseconds -- in bounds by construction,
// and through the bare pointer a `buffer` is indexed by, which no check here
// can bound (`SAFETY.md` S-17b's fourth row). So it is said here.
pub func:host_now_utc = Timestamp() {
    buffer:ts = buffer_new(16i64);
    Result<int64>:r = sys(SYS_CLOCK_GETTIME, CLOCK_REALTIME, ts.ptr);
    if (r.is_error) { fail r.err; }
    int64:secs = <-(#ptr_add<int64>(ts.ptr, 0i64));
    int64:nanos = <-(#ptr_add<int64>(ts.ptr, 1i64));
    pass (relay timestamp_of(secs, nanos));
};

// `HOST.md` H-5: the floor's `mono_now()`, not `clock_gettime` -- the clock the
// deadline substrate reads, so an `Instant` from here and an executor deadline
// are on one timeline by construction. `never fails`, as `mono_now` is, and
// `raw` before `instant_of`, which is `never fails` too: the compiler asks for
// it before a callee whose value is used.
pub func:host_now_instant = Instant() never fails {
    pass raw instant_of(mono_now(), InstantClock.Monotonic);
};

// `HOST.md` H-4 and H-6: `clock_gettime(CLOCK_BOOTTIME)`, which counts
// suspend; the errno forwarded, the `timespec` turned into nanoseconds by
// `timespec_ns`, and the `Instant` tagged `Boottime`, so `instant_since`
// refuses it beside a `Monotonic` reading. The reads, as `host_now_utc`'s.
pub func:host_now_boot = Instant() {
    buffer:ts = buffer_new(16i64);
    Result<int64>:r = sys(SYS_CLOCK_GETTIME, CLOCK_BOOTTIME, ts.ptr);
    if (r.is_error) { fail r.err; }
    int64:secs = <-(#ptr_add<int64>(ts.ptr, 0i64));
    int64:nanos = <-(#ptr_add<int64>(ts.ptr, 1i64));
    int64:ns = relay timespec_ns(secs, nanos);
    pass raw instant_of(ns, InstantClock.Boottime);
};

// `HOST.md` H-1 and H-4: `clock_getres` on the clock `which` names -- the one
// use of `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` here, H-5 holding for the reading -- the errno
// forwarded, and the `timespec` through `timespec_ns` into a `Duration`. The
// reads, as `host_now_utc`'s.
pub func:host_clock_res = Duration(HostClock:which) {
    int64:id = CLOCK_REALTIME;
    pick (which) {
        (HostClock.Realtime)  { id = CLOCK_REALTIME; },
        (HostClock.Monotonic) { id = CLOCK_MONOTONIC; },
        (HostClock.Boottime)  { id = CLOCK_BOOTTIME; }
    }
    buffer:ts = buffer_new(16i64);
    Result<int64>:r = sys(SYS_CLOCK_GETRES, id, ts.ptr);
    if (r.is_error) { fail r.err; }
    int64:secs = <-(#ptr_add<int64>(ts.ptr, 0i64));
    int64:nanos = <-(#ptr_add<int64>(ts.ptr, 1i64));
    int64:ns = relay timespec_ns(secs, nanos);
    pass raw duration_ns(ns);
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The system zone -- `HOST.md` H-12 ... H-16, cycle 0.3.2 (TM-256)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `readlink`, x86-64: H-15's number, and the kernel's table's
// (`arch/x86/entry/syscalls/syscall_64.tbl`, `meta/research/CURRENCY.md`).
fixed int64:SYS_READLINK = 89i64;

// WHICH MECHANISM ANSWERED (H-12, TM-256). The third variant is
// `/etc/timezone`'s: H-12 named it `TzDirLink`, which names no mechanism H-13
// has -- step 3 reads a one-line text file, through no link and no `TZDIR`.
#[derive(Eq, Clone, Debug, Copy)]
pub enum:ZoneSource = { TzEnvironment; EtcLocaltime; EtcTimezone; NotFound; };

// WHAT THE DISCOVERY FOUND, AND WHERE (H-12, TM-256). `name` is the zone's
// NAME, as the mechanism gave it -- H-12's `ZoneId:zone`, whose type and the
// compiled table it indexes are cycles 0.5's and 0.6's. A program turns the
// name into a zone with the lookup (`zone_by_name`, cycle 0.6), whose
// `ETimeZone`/`Unknown` refuses a name the table lacks, a POSIX rule string
// and the empty value among them; so this module looks nothing up, imports
// nothing from `zone`, and raises nothing. `found` is false only with
// `NotFound`, and `name` is then empty -- never UTC (H-13.4).
pub struct:SystemZone = { string:name; ZoneSource:source; bool:found; };

func:zone_none = SystemZone() never fails {
    pass SystemZone{ name: "", source: ZoneSource.NotFound, found: false };
};

// STEP 1, `$TZ` (H-13.1): the first entry of `environ()` that begins `TZ=` --
// the `=` part of the prefix, so `TZX=` is another variable -- read in place,
// as `probe09` measured the entries' bytes are the kernel's and stay. One
// leading `:` is stripped (POSIX), and the rest is the name, VERBATIM: a
// POSIX rule string is reported as the text it is, never parsed (Z-12), and
// the empty value is reported empty. A set `TZ` always answers, because a
// program run with `TZ` set was asked for that zone, and moving on to
// `/etc/localtime` would answer a question the environment did not ask --
// the GNU C library reads an empty `TZ` as UTC
// (`meta/research/tz-environment.md`), and a silent fall to the machine's
// zone would disagree with the C programs beside it. The copy is
// `string_slice`'s, in bounds by construction: `lo` is 3 or 4 and at most
// `eb.len`, so its refusal is `#unreachable()` (`SAFETY.md` S-15c).
func:zone_from_tz = SystemZone() never fails {
    cstring[]:env = environ();
    for (int64:k in 0i64..(env.len - 1i64)) {
        string:entry = string_from_bytes(env[k].ptr, env[k].len);
        fixed uint8[]:eb = string_bytes(entry);
        if (eb.len >= 3i64) {
            if ((eb[0i64] == 84u8) && (eb[1i64] == 90u8) && (eb[2i64] == 61u8)) {   // `TZ=`
                int64:lo = 3i64;
                if (eb.len > 3i64) {
                    if (eb[3i64] == 58u8) { lo = 4i64; }                        // `:`
                }
                string:name = string_slice(entry, lo, eb.len) ?| #unreachable();
                pass SystemZone{ name: move(name), source: ZoneSource.TzEnvironment, found: true };
            }
        }
    }
    pass raw zone_none();
};

// STEP 2, `/etc/localtime`'s link (H-13.2, H-14, H-15): `readlink` into a
// buffer of `NTIME_PATH_MAX` bytes, and the TARGET read, never the file -- a
// regular file is `EINVAL`, and a dangling link still names its zone. H-15's
// four facts, each where it is kept: the RETURNED LENGTH is the authority --
// the view is `n` bytes, and no NUL is looked for, since the kernel writes
// none; the buffer is `NTIME_PATH_MAX`; and a result that fills it may be a
// prefix, so it does not answer (the kernel refuses a link of 4 096 bytes,
// `probe22`, so a link it made never fills it). The name is the tail after
// the LAST `zoneinfo/` that begins the target or follows a `/` -- the last,
// so a path that holds two names the second; at a component's start, so
// `myzoneinfo/` is not one. No such `zoneinfo/`, an empty tail, or any error
// -- no link, a regular file, a path the process cannot search -- and the
// step does not answer. The tail is reported as the target holds it:
// `posix/Europe/London` is the lookup's to judge. The view is of the buffer,
// which outlives it; the copy is `string_slice`'s, `lo` at most `tb.len`.
func:zone_from_localtime = SystemZone() never fails {
    cstring:path = to_cstring("/etc/localtime") ?| #unreachable();
    buffer:b = buffer_new(NTIME_PATH_MAX);
    int64:n = sys(SYS_READLINK, path.ptr, b.ptr, NTIME_PATH_MAX) ?| -1i64;
    if (n <= 0i64) { pass raw zone_none(); }
    if (n >= NTIME_PATH_MAX) { pass raw zone_none(); }                  // truncated
    string:target = string_from_bytes(b.ptr, n);
    fixed uint8[]:tb = string_bytes(target);
    string:mark = "zoneinfo/";
    fixed uint8[]:mb = string_bytes(mark);
    int64:lo = -1i64;
    for (int64:i in 0i64..(tb.len - mb.len)) {
        bool:same = true;
        if (i > 0i64) { same = (tb[i - 1i64] == 47u8); }                // `/`
        for (int64:j in 0i64..(mb.len - 1i64)) {
            if (tb[i + j] != mb[j]) { same = false; }
        }
        if (same) { lo = i + mb.len; }
    }
    if (lo < 0i64) { pass raw zone_none(); }
    if (lo >= tb.len) { pass raw zone_none(); }
    string:name = string_slice(target, lo, tb.len) ?| #unreachable();
    pass SystemZone{ name: move(name), source: ZoneSource.EtcLocaltime, found: true };
};

// STEP 3, `/etc/timezone` (H-13.3, H-16, `SAFETY.md` S-20): the library's one
// descriptor. Opened `O_RDONLY | O_NONBLOCK | O_CLOEXEC` -- `O_NONBLOCK` so a
// FIFO at that path never blocks the open (S-21: `ntime` blocks on nothing),
// `O_CLOEXEC` so the descriptor never outlives the call into an `exec` -- and
// owned at once by an `OwnedFd`, whose drop closes it: every `pass` below is a
// path out, and each one closes it, by construction rather than by a `close`
// on each path. ONE read of up to `NTIME_PATH_MAX` bytes: a read that fills
// the buffer is longer than any name, and does not answer -- a link to
// `/dev/zero` is read once, and is not a name. The name is the bytes before
// the first newline, every other byte reported as the file holds it; an empty
// first line, an empty file (the floor's `E_EOF`), or any error -- no file, a
// directory, a FIFO with no writer -- and the step does not answer.
func:zone_from_timezone = SystemZone() never fails {
    cstring:path = to_cstring("/etc/timezone") ?| #unreachable();
    Result<fd>:o = open(path, ((((O_RDONLY | O_NONBLOCK) | O_CLOEXEC) => int32) => int64),
                        ((S_NONE => int32) => int64));
    if (o.is_error) { pass raw zone_none(); }
    OwnedFd:d = own_fd(o.value);
    buffer:b = buffer_new(NTIME_PATH_MAX);
    int64:n = read(d.value, b.ptr, NTIME_PATH_MAX) ?| -1i64;
    if (n <= 0i64) { pass raw zone_none(); }
    if (n >= NTIME_PATH_MAX) { pass raw zone_none(); }                  // longer than a name
    string:text = string_from_bytes(b.ptr, n);
    fixed uint8[]:fb = string_bytes(text);
    int64:e = fb.len;
    for (int64:i in 0i64..(fb.len - 1i64)) {
        if (fb[i] == 10u8) { e = i; break; }                            // `\n`
    }
    if (e == 0i64) { pass raw zone_none(); }
    string:name = string_slice(text, 0i64, e) ?| #unreachable();
    pass SystemZone{ name: move(name), source: ZoneSource.EtcTimezone, found: true };
};

// `HOST.md` H-12 and H-13: the four steps, in order, stopping at the first
// that answers; the fourth is `NotFound`, `found: false` -- not UTC, and not
// an error (TM-019). `never fails`: a step whose source cannot be read has not
// answered, so nothing is left to fail but what traps -- an allocation, or a
// copy out of bounds that cannot happen. Two calls are two discoveries (H-3):
// nothing is kept between them.
pub func:host_system_zone = SystemZone() never fails {
    SystemZone:z = raw zone_from_tz();
    if (z.found) { pass z; }
    z = raw zone_from_localtime();
    if (z.found) { pass z; }
    z = raw zone_from_timezone();
    if (z.found) { pass z; }
    pass raw zone_none();
};
